* emacs-tessera

~emacs-tessera~ is a modern interface suite for ~elfeed~, ~gnus~, and ~mu4e~.

** Tessera Gnus

Tessera Gnus currently provides pixel-aligned interfaces for ~gnus-group-mode~ and
~gnus-summary-mode~, including native Gnus topics.  It changes presentation and
interaction only; Gnus continues to own groups, topics, articles, marks, scores,
and persistent state.

#+HTML: <p align="center"><img width="80%" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/868540b4-6eb4-4ff7-925c-15a9dc5c3ac5" alt="Tessera Gnus Group and Summary"><br><sub>Tessera Gnus displaying a Topic Group and threaded Summary.</sub></p>

The screenshot uses [[https://protesilaos.com/emacs/modus-themes][Modus Themes]] and [[https://mbtype.com/fonts/triplicate/][Triplicate Code]].

*** Install and Configure

Tessera requires Emacs 30.1 or later.  The core and Gnus integration are
separate packages in this repository, so install both:

#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(use-package tessera
  :vc (:url "https://github.com/brsvh/emacs-tessera.git"
       :lisp-dir "lisp/tessera"))

(use-package tessera-gnus
  :after tessera
  :vc (:url "https://github.com/brsvh/emacs-tessera.git"
       :lisp-dir "lisp/tessera-gnus")
  :config
  (tessera-gnus-mode 1))
#+end_src

~tessera-gnus-mode~ is global, but enabling it does not start Gnus.  It installs
the interface when Gnus becomes available and updates active Group and Summary
buffers.  Disabling it restores their native presentation.

*** Customize

Use ~M-x customize-group RET tessera-gnus RET~ to inspect the available options.
They cover topics, glyphs, feature categories, semantic dates, and the
presentation refresh delay.  Changing an option through Customize redraws the
affected Tessera buffers.

**** Threaded Summaries

Tessera follows the native ~gnus-show-threads~ option.  Set it before entering or
regenerating a Summary buffer:

#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(setq gnus-show-threads t)            ; Threaded Summary.
;; (setq gnus-show-threads nil)       ; Flat Summary.
#+end_src

Threading changes only the Summary organization.  Tessera derives the tree,
thread counts, and marks from Gnus rather than maintaining a second thread
model.

#+HTML: <p align="center"><img width="49%" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/4baf66ac-b5d8-4e0e-91c9-d8148ddd37cb" alt="Threaded Gnus Summary"><img width="49%" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/93d2df69-f7eb-4eaf-abc8-52e052ca5297" alt="Flat Gnus Summary"><br><sub>Threaded Summary (left) and Flat Summary (right).</sub></p>

**** Topics

Tessera follows the native ~gnus-topic-mode~, topic topology, and folding state.
Topic levels are shown with progressively smaller text instead of horizontal
indentation; the root topic serves as the main Group heading.

Collapsed topics show a disclosure indicator and right-aligned unread and group
counts.  Expanded topics omit the indicator and statistics.  Clicking a topic
uses the native Gnus folding command.

By default, TAB retains its native ~gnus-topic-indent~ behavior.  Set
~tessera-gnus-topic-tab-fold~ to non-nil to make TAB fold or expand a topic when
point is on its row:

#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(setq tessera-gnus-topic-tab-fold t)
#+end_src

**** Symbols and Icons

The default glyph style is ~unicode~.  Set the Gnus default with ~setq-default~,
because individual Gnus buffers may override it:

#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(setq-default tessera-gnus-symbol-style 'unicode)
#+end_src

The accepted values are ~native~, ~unicode~, and ~nerd-icons~.  The ~nerd-icons~ style
is optional and requires the ~nerd-icons~ package and its fonts; unavailable icons
fall back to ordinary symbols.

#+HTML: <p align="center"><img width="32%" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/cbf0617f-cd31-44aa-b05b-14a3956d9906" alt="Native Gnus Summary symbols"><img width="32%" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/0797cd6b-4768-4d88-93dc-8ae376bf867a" alt="Unicode Gnus Summary symbols"><img width="32%" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/4baf66ac-b5d8-4e0e-91c9-d8148ddd37cb" alt="Nerd Icons Gnus Summary symbols"><br><sub>Native (left), Unicode (center), and Nerd Icons (right).</sub></p>

Use ~tessera-gnus-summary-mark-symbol-alist~ and
~tessera-gnus-summary-feature-symbol-alist~ to override individual glyphs:

#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(setq tessera-gnus-summary-mark-symbol-alist
      '((gnus-ticked-mark . "★")))
(setq tessera-gnus-summary-feature-symbol-alist
      '((attachment . "▣")))
#+end_src

Use ~tessera-gnus-group-mark-symbol-alist~ to override the Group process and
subscription marks.

Marks are the leading glyphs for native Gnus article state, such as unread,
ticked, dormant, workflow, and score marks.  Features are the glyphs following
the author for content properties such as encryption, signatures, attachments,
calendar data, audience, and overflow.  Tessera presents these facts without
storing another copy of them.

Set ~tessera-gnus-glyph-color-style~ to nil to make their foregrounds follow the
surrounding entry.  In a flat Summary, marks follow the subject while features
follow the author.  In a threaded Summary, both marks and features follow the
author of their article.  Only the foreground follows; text weight and slant do
not.  Group marks use one neutral mark face because they have no subject or
author.

Set the option to t to retain colors associated with each semantic role, or set
it to a color string to apply one foreground to every Gnus mark and feature.

#+HTML: <p align="center"><img width="32%" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/4baf66ac-b5d8-4e0e-91c9-d8148ddd37cb" alt="Semantic Gnus glyph colors"><img width="32%" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/2effd23a-1e92-44c6-8882-5100ded3c9a3" alt="Contextual Gnus glyph colors"><img width="32%" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/f0ba3e6c-76ca-46b3-9573-be1916f75a59" alt="Fixed Gnus glyph color"><br><sub>Semantic colors (<code>t</code>, left), contextual colors (<code>nil</code>, center), and a fixed <code>#4169E1</code> foreground (right).</sub></p>

**** Notifications

Set ~tessera-gnus-notify-enable~ to ~t~ to enable Gnus notifications independently,
or to nil to disable them.  Its default value, ~inherit~, follows the global
setting.

Updates containing at most ~tessera-gnus-notify-limit~ new unread articles send
one notification per article; the default limit is four.  Larger updates send
one summary without actions.  Individual notifications provide ~Read~ and ~Mark as
Read~ actions backed by native Gnus operations.

Individual notifications use the shared sender avatar support and fall back to
the Gnus icon.

