** Tessera

Tessera is the common foundation shared by the backend packages.  It defines the
common UI vocabulary, semantic faces, pixel-aware layout helpers, and
notification dependency; it does not replace any backend data model.

*** Install and Configure

Backend packages depend on Tessera.  It may also be installed independently:

#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(use-package tessera
  :vc (:url "https://github.com/brsvh/emacs-tessera.git"
       :lisp-dir "lisp/tessera"))
#+end_src

There is no global Tessera mode.  Enable the global mode for each backend you
want Tessera to present, such as ~tessera-gnus-mode~.

*** Customize

Use ~M-x customize-group RET tessera RET~ for shared faces.  Faces such as
~tessera-entry-subject~, ~tessera-entry-author~, and ~tessera-thread-connector~
currently affect Tessera Gnus and are intended to remain consistent across
future backends.

**** Backend Symbols and Icons

The core package does not impose a glyph style.  Configure symbols and icons
through the backend option; for Gnus, use ~tessera-gnus-symbol-style~ and the
override alists described above.

**** Notifications

Notifications are globally disabled by default.  Backend notification options
follow ~tessera-notify-enable~ unless explicitly overridden, and the global value
may be changed at any time.

Tessera maintains a shared cache of sender Gravatar images.  Set
~tessera-notify-use-gravatar~ to ~nil~ to disable sender avatars, or set
~tessera-notify-use-icons~ to ~nil~ to disable notification icons.

** Build

The build requires GNU Make, Emacs 30.1 or later, and ~tar~.  Build both
packages with:

#+begin_src sh
make
#+end_src

Use ~make tessera~ or ~make tessera-gnus~ to build one package.  The build generates
package descriptions, autoloads, byte-compiled files, and validated source
archives under ~dist/~.  Run ~make clean~ to remove generated files.

** Test

There is no automated test suite yet.  ~make~ treats byte-compilation warnings as
errors and validates the metadata in each generated package archive.

For interactive inspection, first make Tessera and the packages required by
~test/init.el.in~ available to Emacs.  Then create the local test configuration
and start Emacs:

#+begin_src sh
mkdir -p local
test -e local/init.el || cp test/init.el.in local/init.el
emacs --init-directory "$PWD/local"
#+end_src

Run ~M-x tessera-gnus-fixture-open~ to open the representative local NNML group.
The fixture definition is tracked under ~test/fixtures/~; generated articles and
Gnus state remain under ~local/fixtures/gnus/~.

